James, Aaron —

Field notes from this week's calls, for the workflow SLAs. Take these as
what plans SAY, not what governs — James's point about the statutes stands
and I'd build the workflow on those where they apply.

The one that will raise eyebrows, from my call with the regional plan's
provider services line Thursday:
The plan's provider rep told me to expect about 15 business days for a standard PA determination on a new specialty product.
Verbatim, and she repeated it when I asked her to confirm. That is
the plan's internal working number for anything without an established
policy on file, and it is exactly the kind of quote that ends up in a
prescriber's front-office folklore if we don't get ahead of it.

Other color for the failure-path design:

- Two of the larger regional plans route any new-to-market biologic to
  medical director review, and both said a peer-to-peer is effectively
  mandatory on first denial — figure a 5 to 8 business day add before the
  P2P gets scheduled.
- The MA-PD plans I spoke with pend routinely for chart notes: flare
  documentation, prior systemic therapy, and (for one of them) a dermatology
  attestation. Pends restart their internal clock, which is how a
  "seven-day" determination becomes three weeks unless the submission
  packet is complete on day one.
- One plan's rep volunteered that expedited requests marked urgent by the
  prescriber clear in 48-72 hours "when the urgency is documented" — GPP
  flares should qualify on their face, so the workflow should default acute
  presentations to the expedited lane with the documentation attached.

Net for the workflow: build the packet checklist so nothing pends, mark
acute cases expedited, and treat any plan-quoted SLA longer than the
statutory clock as a compliance escalation, not a fact of life.

Tanya

